{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794523916384,"sessionID":"ses_e2e0e4d38ffeP1JOC1np1mhw9O","part":{"partID":"prt_1d1f5e63e001dsvt1nVsSJpBjO","sessionID":"ses_e2e0e4d38ffeP1JOC1np1mhw9O","messageID":"msg_1d1f5d340001dWUGm0GXPKK1kW","type":"tool","id":"call_4021df13ec3241b1ba11f4f1","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research && sed -n '1320,1420p' .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/skill-advisor-cli.ts"},"output":"  }\n}\n\n// The deep probe doubles as the daemon's handshake, and its failure reason splits the\n// two cases the cold-start budget must tell apart. A timeout means the daemon took the\n// connection and stayed silent, the one failure still worth waiting out on the caller's\n// own timeout. Every other reason — absent socket, refused connection, a handshake that\n// errored or closed before replying — is a daemon that is simply not running yet, so the\n// call starts it and waits, bounded: a caller that can also answer without the daemon\n// must not be held for the whole tool timeout by one that never accepts a connection.\nfunction probeTimedOut(probe: BridgeProbeResult): boolean {\n  return /timeout/i.test(probe.reason ?? '');\n}\n\nasync function ensureDaemonReady(\n  socketPath: string,\n  bridge: BridgeModule,\n  paths: RepoPaths,\n  timeoutMs: number,\n  warmOnly: boolean,\n  hasLocalFallback: boolean,\n): Promise<void> {\n  const initialProbe = await bridge.probeDaemon(socketPath, { timeoutMs: Math.min(timeoutMs, 5000), deepProbe: true });\n  if (initialProbe.status === 'alive') {\n    if (warmOnly || !liveDaemonPredatesBuild(paths)) return;\n    await waitForStaleDaemonRecycle(paths, timeoutMs, spawnLauncher(paths));\n    await waitForDaemon(socketPath, bridge, timeoutMs);\n    return;\n  }\n  if (warmOnly) {\n    throw new CliRetryableError(`backend unavailable: ${initialProbe.reason ?? initialProbe.status}`);\n  }\n  const waitMs = hasLocalFallback && !probeTimedOut(initialProbe)\n    ? Math.min(timeoutMs, COLD_START_WAIT_MS)\n    : timeoutMs;\n  await waitForDaemon(socketPath, bridge, waitMs, spawnLauncher(paths));\n}\n\nasync function callTool(toolName: string, args: Record<string, unknown>, timeoutMs: number, trusted: boolean, warmOnly: boolean): Promise<unknown> {\n  ensureSocketEnvironment();\n  const paths = findRepoPaths();\n  const bridge = loadBridge(paths);\n  const socketPath = bridge.getIpcSocketPath('system-skill-advisor', { dbDir: paths.dbDir });\n  if (socketPathTooLong(socketPath)) {\n    throw new CliProtocolError(`IPC socket path exceeds the Darwin sun_path limit: ${socketPath}`);\n  }\n\n  // advisor_recommend carries a local-scorer fallback, so it starts the daemon and waits\n  // for it only within the bounded cold-start window; the local scorer still answers if\n  // that window closes. Tools without a fallback keep the caller's full timeout: for them\n  // the daemon is the only answer.\n  await ensureDaemonReady(socketPath, bridge, paths, timeoutMs, warmOnly, toolName === 'advisor_recommend');\n\n  const client = await JsonRpcSocketClient.connect(socketPath, bridge, timeoutMs);\n  try {\n    const initialize = await client.request('initialize', {\n      clientInfo: { name: 'skill-advisor-cli', version: readCliVersion(paths) },\n      advisorProtocol: ADVISOR_PROTOCOL_VERSION,\n    }, timeoutMs) as InitializeResult;\n    if (initialize.advisorProtocol !== ADVISOR_PROTOCOL_VERSION) {\n      const daemonVersion = initialize.advisorProtocol ?? 'missing advisorProtocol';\n      throw new CliProtocolError(\n        `backend protocol mismatch: daemon \"${daemonVersion}\", client \"${ADVISOR_PROTOCOL_VERSION}\"`,\n      );\n    }\n    return await client.request('advisor.call', {\n      command: toolName,\n      args,\n      _meta: callerMeta(trusted),\n    }, timeoutMs);\n  } finally {\n    client.close();\n  }\n}\n\nasync function invokeToolPayload(toolName: string, args: Record<string, unknown>, timeoutMs: number, trusted: boolean, warmOnly: boolean): Promise<ToolPayloadResult> {\n  const result = await callTool(toolName, args, timeoutMs, trusted, warmOnly);\n  return extractToolPayload(result);\n}\n\nasync function invokeWithRetry<T>(operation: () => Promise<T>, options: RetryOptions): Promise<T> {\n  let attempt = 1;\n  while (true) {\n    try {\n      return await operation();\n    } catch (error: unknown) {\n      if (attempt >= options.maxAttempts || !isRetryableError(error)) throw error;\n      await (options.sleepFn ?? sleep)(options.sleepMsForAttempt(attempt));\n      attempt += 1;\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nasync function invokeAdvisorValidatePayload(args: Record<string, unknown>, timeoutMs: number, trusted: boolean, warmOnly: boolean): Promise<ToolPayloadResult> {\n  return invokeWithRetry(\n    () => invokeToolPayload('advisor_validate', args, timeoutMs, trusted, warmOnly),\n    {\n      maxAttempts: ADVISOR_VALIDATE_MAX_ATTEMPTS,\n      sleepMsForAttempt: advisorValidateRetryDelayMs,\n    },\n  );\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"  }\n}\n\n// The deep probe doubles as the daemon's handshake, and its failure reason splits the\n// two cases the cold-start budget must tell apart. A timeout means the daemon took the\n// connection and stayed silent, the one failure still worth waiting out on the caller's\n// own timeout. Every other reason — absent socket, refused connection, a handshake that\n// errored or closed before replying — is a daemon that is simply not running yet, so the\n// call starts it and waits, bounded: a caller that can also answer without the daemon\n// must not be held for the whole tool timeout by one that never accepts a connection.\nfunction probeTimedOut(probe: BridgeProbeResult): boolean {\n  return /timeout/i.test(probe.reason ?? '');\n}\n\nasync function ensureDaemonReady(\n  socketPath: string,\n  bridge: BridgeModule,\n  paths: RepoPaths,\n  timeoutMs: number,\n  warmOnly: boolean,\n  hasLocalFallback: boolean,\n): Promise<void> {\n  const initialProbe = await bridge.probeDaemon(socketPath, { timeoutMs: Math.min(timeoutMs, 5000), deepProbe: true });\n  if (initialProbe.status === 'alive') {\n    if (warmOnly || !liveDaemonPredatesBuild(paths)) return;\n    await waitForStaleDaemonRecycle(paths, timeoutMs, spawnLauncher(paths));\n    await waitForDaemon(socketPath, bridge, timeoutMs);\n    return;\n  }\n  if (warmOnly) {\n    throw new CliRetryableError(`backend unavailable: ${initialProbe.reason ?? initialProbe.status}`);\n  }\n  const waitMs = hasLocalFallback && !probeTimedOut(initialProbe)\n    ? Math.min(timeoutMs, COLD_START_WAIT_MS)\n    : timeoutMs;\n  await waitForDaemon(socketPath, bridge, waitMs, spawnLauncher(paths));\n}\n\nasync function callTool(toolName: string, args: Record<string, unknown>, timeoutMs: number, trusted: boolean, warmOnly: boolean): Promise<unknown> {\n  ensureSocketEnvironment();\n  const paths = findRepoPaths();\n  const bridge = loadBridge(paths);\n  const socketPath = bridge.getIpcSocketPath('system-skill-advisor', { dbDir: paths.dbDir });\n  if (socketPathTooLong(socketPath)) {\n    throw new CliProtocolError(`IPC socket path exceeds the Darwin sun_path limit: ${socketPath}`);\n  }\n\n  // advisor_recommend carries a local-scorer fallback, so it starts the daemon and waits\n  // for it only within the bounded cold-start window; the local scorer still answers if\n  // that window closes. Tools without a fallback keep the caller's full timeout: for them\n  // the daemon is the only answer.\n  await ensureDaemonReady(socketPath, bridge, paths, timeoutMs, warmOnly, toolName === 'advisor_recommend');\n\n  const client = await JsonRpcSocketClient.connect(socketPath, bridge, timeoutMs);\n  try {\n    const initialize = await client.request('initialize', {\n      clientInfo: { name: 'skill-advisor-cli', version: readCliVersion(paths) },\n      advisorProtocol: ADVISOR_PROTOCOL_VERSION,\n    }, timeoutMs) as InitializeResult;\n    if (initialize.advisorProtocol !== ADVISOR_PROTOCOL_VERSION) {\n      const daemonVersion = initialize.advisorProtocol ?? 'missing advisorProtocol';\n      throw new CliProtocolError(\n        `backend protocol mismatch: daemon \"${daemonVersion}\", client \"${ADVISOR_PROTOCOL_VERSION}\"`,\n      );\n    }\n    return await client.request('advisor.call', {\n      command: toolName,\n      args,\n      _meta: callerMeta(trusted),\n    }, timeoutMs);\n  } finally {\n    client.close();\n  }\n}\n\nasync function invokeToolPayload(toolName: string, args: Record<string, unknown>, timeoutMs: number, trusted: boolean, warmOnly: boolean): Promise<ToolPayloadResult> {\n  const result = await callTool(toolName, args, timeoutMs, trusted, warmOnly);\n  return extractToolPayload(result);\n}\n\nasync function invokeWithRetry<T>(operation: () => Promise<T>, options: RetryOptions): Promise<T> {\n  let attempt = 1;\n  while (true) {\n    try {\n      return await operation();\n    } catch (error: unknown) {\n      if (attempt >= options.maxAttempts || !isRetryableError(error)) throw error;\n      await (options.sleepFn ?? sleep)(options.sleepMsForAttempt(attempt));\n      attempt += 1;\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nasync function invokeAdvisorValidatePayload(args: Record<string, unknown>, timeoutMs: number, trusted: boolean, warmOnly: boolean): Promise<ToolPayloadResult> {\n  return invokeWithRetry(\n    () => invokeToolPayload('advisor_validate', args, timeoutMs, trusted, warmOnly),\n    {\n      maxAttempts: ADVISOR_VALIDATE_MAX_ATTEMPTS,\n      sleepMsForAttempt: advisorValidateRetryDelayMs,\n    },\n  );\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\":\"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research && sed -n '1320,1420p' .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/skill-advisor-cli.ts\"}"},"time":{"start":1794523915838,"end":1794523916384}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794523916416,"sessionID":"ses_e2e0e4d38ffeP1JOC1np1mhw9O","part":{"partID":"prt_1d1f5e1fa001lY25mKhaHAelcY","sessionID":"ses_e2e0e4d38ffeP1JOC1np1mhw9O","messageID":"msg_1d1f5d340001dWUGm0GXPKK1kW","type":"tool","id":"call_54760f310952486e9ef848a0","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research && rg -rn \"cold start|COLD_START_WAIT|daemon start|launcher\" .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/*.md .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/*.md specs/system-skill-advisor --glob '*.md' 2>/dev/null | grep -i \"ms\\|second\\|cold\" | head -20"},"output":".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/INSTALL-GUIDE.md:Prompt-time surfaces run the same scorer, which is why a shim smoke test doubles as a hook smoke test. There is no plugin bridge or MCP client in the chain: the hooks and the OpenCode plugin shell out to `.skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs`, and the CLI starts the daemon through `.skilled/bin/system-skill-advisor-n.cjs` when the socket is cold.\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/013-review-follow-ups/spec.md:- **F1 (P1), scenario 433's teardown.** `system-spec-kit/manual-testing-playbook/ux-hooks/cli-hook-transport-down-fail-open.md:61-78` matches the lease socket path as text, sends `kill -TERM` to the lease pids and runs `rm -rf` without waiting for the daemon. GPT-6 Luna failed this design twice in phase 12 (`../012-reverify-follow-ups/evidence/luna-verify/report-1.txt`, `report-2.txt`). The default-budget hook call starts a sandbox n on every run, as all five phase 11 reports show. 433 gets the signal-free wait with a 12-second idle timeout. Three scenarios point the advisor database at a sandbox, and 433 is the only one left that signals.\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/013-review-follow-ups/spec.md:- **F2, case B's proof.** Case B's daemon exited at 16.6 seconds, before the wait's 25-second floor. The wait without the open-file test would therefore have removed that sandbox at the same moment (`../012-reverify-follow-ups/evidence/teardown-check/open-file-result.txt:21`). So the claim that the open-file test covers a crashed n rests on code reading and case A2. On the operator's yes, one watched crash test kills a sandbox n. It then samples the sandbox socket, the daemon's open database and the live advisor every 0.1 seconds.\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/013-review-follow-ups/spec.md:- **The live advisor's restart at 07:51:42Z.** The monitor recorded the live daemon shutting down on a SIGTERM twice within two seconds while no sandbox existed and no command of this phase sent a signal. The likely path is the n's dead-socket respawn, which is advisor runtime code. It is recorded in `evidence/live-advisor-restart.txt` for the operator and not changed here.\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/013-review-follow-ups/spec.md:| REQ-004 | The crash path has a direct observation | The watched crash test kills its own sandbox n only after checking the lease socket, the pid and the command. It then records at 0.1-second resolution when the sandbox socket goes, when the daemon's database closes and when the daemon exits, along with the live advisor's pid throughout |\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/013-review-follow-ups/spec.md:| Risk | The watched crash test kills a sandbox n, and phase 12's third live advisor restart happened while case B ran | Med | It kills only a n whose lease socket lies inside its sandbox, whose pid is not a live advisor pid and whose command is a n. It samples the live advisor's pid every 0.1 seconds, so any change is timed |\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/013-review-follow-ups/plan.md:- **Scenario 433's n**: the block exports `SPECKIT_LAUNCHER_IDLE_TIMEOUT_MIN=0.2` with its sandbox variables, so the hook's CLI hands the 12-second idle timeout to the sandbox n it starts, and unsets all four at the end.\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/013-review-follow-ups/plan.md:- **Watched crash test**: cold-starts a sandbox, kills its n once the checks pass and removes both lease files as the crash handler would. It then samples every 0.1 seconds until the daemon exits.\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/013-review-follow-ups/goal.md:| Live advisor restart | At 07:51:42Z the monitor recorded live daemon 87539 shutting down on a SIGTERM, then a new daemon doing the same 0.67 seconds after its scan. Launcher 21222 and daemon 21257 have served since. No sandbox existed, no command of this phase sent a signal and D2 held. The likely path is the n's dead-socket respawn, which is outside this phase (`evidence/live-advisor-restart.txt`) |\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/013-review-follow-ups/implementation-summary.md:A fresh Opus 5.5 review of phase 12 found one P1 and nine P2 problems, and the orchestrator confirmed all but one against the files. Scenario 433 no longer signals a process to clear its sandbox. The three sandbox teardowns now keep a folder they cannot check instead of removing it. One watched crash test observed what the crashed-n claim had only inferred, and the phase 12 records say only what their evidence shows. The reruns found one more problem: the generation check in 433 and CP-004 can read the live advisor's own reindex as a sandbox leak.\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/013-review-follow-ups/implementation-summary.md:**The watched crash test (F2).** On the operator's yes, one run killed a sandbox n after its cold-start call and removed its lease files, as the n's crash handler would. Samples every 0.1 seconds showed the orphaned daemon holding its databases and its socket until +15.63 seconds. At +15.74 seconds it held nothing in the sandbox while its socket file stayed, and at +15.85 seconds it was gone with the socket folder empty. So the lease-and-socket test alone would also have waited for this daemon, and the open-file test covered no extra time in this run. The result changed no block. It corrected phase 12's claim instead, and the live advisor stayed up in all 186 samples.\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/013-review-follow-ups/implementation-summary.md:6. **Four advisor changes during the phase have no confirmed cause.** Launcher 33066 gave way to 87524 at 06:47:40Z, before this phase's first check, and generation 562 came from the new n's startup scan. Worktree 069's n 9479 and daemon 9492 were gone by 07:24:53Z. At 07:51:42Z live daemon 87539 shut down on a SIGTERM and a new daemon did the same 0.67 seconds after its scan. Launcher 21222 with daemon 21257 has served since. The n's dead-socket respawn is the likely path, but the live n keeps no log to show it. Barter's n 86318 and daemon 86320, listed at 07:50:21Z, were gone by 07:55:30Z. No command of this phase named any of those advisors' sockets or databases or sent them a signal (`evidence/live-advisor-change.txt`, `evidence/live-advisor-restart.txt`, `evidence/reruns/state-before.txt`).\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/013-review-follow-ups/evidence/reruns/quiet-reruns/reports/devin-433.md:NOTES: The sandbox daemon/n apparently never fully started within the CLI's cold-start window — both work-payload runs degraded to `CLI fallback timed out` (exit 75 → fail_open), so `default:` rendered the outage line rather than `live;`/`stale;`. All three `default:` outcomes are a documented pass. The teardown's no-signal wait still confirmed nothing held the sandbox (`lsof +D` clean) before removing it.\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/006-fanout-deep-review/review/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-002.md:- **IPC socket directory hardening (out of scope)**: `.skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs:91` creates the socket dir with `mode: 0o700` but `mkdirSync(recursive)` does not repair an attacker-pre-created directory, and `.skilled/bin/lib/n-ipc-bridge.cjs` has no chmod. Both files are outside `goal-file-manifest.txt`, so this is recorded as an adjacent-surface observation for the deferred-items section, not a finding.\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/009-test-findings-remediation/spec.md:- **F21** `classifyOwnerLease` in `.skilled/bin/system-skill-advisor-n.cjs` reclaims a live owner lease as `ppid-1-orphan` whenever the owner's parent is pid 1 and differs from the parent it recorded. The CLI starts every n detached, so a healthy n is reparented to pid 1 as soon as the CLI exits. A second n on the same database then reclaims the lease, finds the live n record, bridges to it and deletes the owner lease. The healthy owner's next heartbeat finds no lease, and it shuts itself and its daemon down. The rule is not in the documented contract (`references/runtime/daemon-lease-contract.md` section 4 names a dead pid, a stale heartbeat or an unverifiable owner). An isolated reproduction with its own database showed the incumbent dead 25 s after a second n call, and scenario 433, which cold-starts a n on a sandbox socket against the live database, displaced the live advisor during the reruns at 22:25Z and 22:41Z.\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/009-test-findings-remediation/spec.md:| REQ-008 | A second n never displaces a live owner | A n test shows a live owner with a fresh heartbeat and parent pid 1 stays `live-owner`, and the isolated two-n reproduction keeps the incumbent alive past two heartbeat intervals |\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/013-review-follow-ups/evidence/reruns/reports/codex-CP-004.md:| 4 | Native n with 0.2-minute idle timeout | 0 | `status: ok`, `freshness: live`; `cold-start exit=0`. | Sandbox daemon returns an OK envelope with live freshness and exit 0. | Yes |\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/009-test-findings-remediation/tasks.md:- [x] T014 F17: the daemon child reaches the model server the n arms (`.skilled/bin/system-skill-advisor-n.cjs`), after T012 verifies. Evidence: n tests 38 of 38 with two new; with the new block removed, 4 tests fail.\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/009-test-findings-remediation/tasks.md:- [x] T022 F21: a second n never reclaims a live, heartbeating owner for its parent pid (`.skilled/bin/system-skill-advisor-n.cjs`, `runtime/tests/n-reap-pid-reuse.vitest.ts`). Evidence: the four n test files 47 of 47 outside the Codex sandbox; with the base n restored, the new test fails on `ownerLeaseReclaimed: ppid-1-orphan` and `expected true to be false`; the test's sleeper has parent pid 1. The isolated two-n reproduction killed the incumbent by t+25 s before the fix and kept it alive through t+60 s after it, with the live n pid and live files unchanged. Luna's diff, caller and test-shape checks passed. Its gate hit the Codex sandbox's socket and `ps` limits, so the orchestrator ran that gate.\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/011-observation-fixes/evidence/goal-reverify/reports/pi-433.md:NOTES: Teardown output verbatim: `sandbox n 34895 stopped` then `live generation file unchanged` — the lease file existed and its socket path was inside the sandbox, so the sandbox n (PID 34895) was TERMed and confirmed stopped; no live daemon or database was touched. The default-budget run landed on the `outage (fail_open)` variant (2508 ms, under the ~5 s cold window) rather than `live`/`stale`; the scenario file states all three variants are a pass. Hook diagnostic JSON went to stderr, stdout held only the JSON payloads — stdout was non-empty in all three runs. The sandbox dir was removed and no repository files were written. Flakiness: none; single run, first attempt.\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/INSTALL-GUIDE.md:Prompt-time surfaces run the same scorer, which is why a shim smoke test doubles as a hook smoke test. There is no plugin bridge or MCP client in the chain: the hooks and the OpenCode plugin shell out to `.skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs`, and the CLI starts the daemon through `.skilled/bin/system-skill-advisor-n.cjs` when the socket is cold.\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/013-review-follow-ups/spec.md:- **F1 (P1), scenario 433's teardown.** `system-spec-kit/manual-testing-playbook/ux-hooks/cli-hook-transport-down-fail-open.md:61-78` matches the lease socket path as text, sends `kill -TERM` to the lease pids and runs `rm -rf` without waiting for the daemon. GPT-6 Luna failed this design twice in phase 12 (`../012-reverify-follow-ups/evidence/luna-verify/report-1.txt`, `report-2.txt`). The default-budget hook call starts a sandbox n on every run, as all five phase 11 reports show. 433 gets the signal-free wait with a 12-second idle timeout. Three scenarios point the advisor database at a sandbox, and 433 is the only one left that signals.\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/013-review-follow-ups/spec.md:- **F2, case B's proof.** Case B's daemon exited at 16.6 seconds, before the wait's 25-second floor. The wait without the open-file test would therefore have removed that sandbox at the same moment (`../012-reverify-follow-ups/evidence/teardown-check/open-file-result.txt:21`). So the claim that the open-file test covers a crashed n rests on code reading and case A2. On the operator's yes, one watched crash test kills a sandbox n. It then samples the sandbox socket, the daemon's open database and the live advisor every 0.1 seconds.\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/013-review-follow-ups/spec.md:- **The live advisor's restart at 07:51:42Z.** The monitor recorded the live daemon shutting down on a SIGTERM twice within two seconds while no sandbox existed and no command of this phase sent a signal. The likely path is the n's dead-socket respawn, which is advisor runtime code. It is recorded in `evidence/live-advisor-restart.txt` for the operator and not changed here.\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/013-review-follow-ups/spec.md:| REQ-004 | The crash path has a direct observation | The watched crash test kills its own sandbox n only after checking the lease socket, the pid and the command. It then records at 0.1-second resolution when the sandbox socket goes, when the daemon's database closes and when the daemon exits, along with the live advisor's pid throughout |\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/013-review-follow-ups/spec.md:| Risk | The watched crash test kills a sandbox n, and phase 12's third live advisor restart happened while case B ran | Med | It kills only a n whose lease socket lies inside its sandbox, whose pid is not a live advisor pid and whose command is a n. It samples the live advisor's pid every 0.1 seconds, so any change is timed |\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/013-review-follow-ups/plan.md:- **Scenario 433's n**: the block exports `SPECKIT_LAUNCHER_IDLE_TIMEOUT_MIN=0.2` with its sandbox variables, so the hook's CLI hands the 12-second idle timeout to the sandbox n it starts, and unsets all four at the end.\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/013-review-follow-ups/plan.md:- **Watched crash test**: cold-starts a sandbox, kills its n once the checks pass and removes both lease files as the crash handler would. It then samples every 0.1 seconds until the daemon exits.\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/013-review-follow-ups/goal.md:| Live advisor restart | At 07:51:42Z the monitor recorded live daemon 87539 shutting down on a SIGTERM, then a new daemon doing the same 0.67 seconds after its scan. Launcher 21222 and daemon 21257 have served since. No sandbox existed, no command of this phase sent a signal and D2 held. The likely path is the n's dead-socket respawn, which is outside this phase (`evidence/live-advisor-restart.txt`) |\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/013-review-follow-ups/implementation-summary.md:A fresh Opus 5.5 review of phase 12 found one P1 and nine P2 problems, and the orchestrator confirmed all but one against the files. Scenario 433 no longer signals a process to clear its sandbox. The three sandbox teardowns now keep a folder they cannot check instead of removing it. One watched crash test observed what the crashed-n claim had only inferred, and the phase 12 records say only what their evidence shows. The reruns found one more problem: the generation check in 433 and CP-004 can read the live advisor's own reindex as a sandbox leak.\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/013-review-follow-ups/implementation-summary.md:**The watched crash test (F2).** On the operator's yes, one run killed a sandbox n after its cold-start call and removed its lease files, as the n's crash handler would. Samples every 0.1 seconds showed the orphaned daemon holding its databases and its socket until +15.63 seconds. At +15.74 seconds it held nothing in the sandbox while its socket file stayed, and at +15.85 seconds it was gone with the socket folder empty. So the lease-and-socket test alone would also have waited for this daemon, and the open-file test covered no extra time in this run. The result changed no block. It corrected phase 12's claim instead, and the live advisor stayed up in all 186 samples.\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/013-review-follow-ups/implementation-summary.md:6. **Four advisor changes during the phase have no confirmed cause.** Launcher 33066 gave way to 87524 at 06:47:40Z, before this phase's first check, and generation 562 came from the new n's startup scan. Worktree 069's n 9479 and daemon 9492 were gone by 07:24:53Z. At 07:51:42Z live daemon 87539 shut down on a SIGTERM and a new daemon did the same 0.67 seconds after its scan. Launcher 21222 with daemon 21257 has served since. The n's dead-socket respawn is the likely path, but the live n keeps no log to show it. Barter's n 86318 and daemon 86320, listed at 07:50:21Z, were gone by 07:55:30Z. No command of this phase named any of those advisors' sockets or databases or sent them a signal (`evidence/live-advisor-change.txt`, `evidence/live-advisor-restart.txt`, `evidence/reruns/state-before.txt`).\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/013-review-follow-ups/evidence/reruns/quiet-reruns/reports/devin-433.md:NOTES: The sandbox daemon/n apparently never fully started within the CLI's cold-start window — both work-payload runs degraded to `CLI fallback timed out` (exit 75 → fail_open), so `default:` rendered the outage line rather than `live;`/`stale;`. All three `default:` outcomes are a documented pass. The teardown's no-signal wait still confirmed nothing held the sandbox (`lsof +D` clean) before removing it.\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/006-fanout-deep-review/review/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-002.md:- **IPC socket directory hardening (out of scope)**: `.skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs:91` creates the socket dir with `mode: 0o700` but `mkdirSync(recursive)` does not repair an attacker-pre-created directory, and `.skilled/bin/lib/n-ipc-bridge.cjs` has no chmod. Both files are outside `goal-file-manifest.txt`, so this is recorded as an adjacent-surface observation for the deferred-items section, not a finding.\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/009-test-findings-remediation/spec.md:- **F21** `classifyOwnerLease` in `.skilled/bin/system-skill-advisor-n.cjs` reclaims a live owner lease as `ppid-1-orphan` whenever the owner's parent is pid 1 and differs from the parent it recorded. The CLI starts every n detached, so a healthy n is reparented to pid 1 as soon as the CLI exits. A second n on the same database then reclaims the lease, finds the live n record, bridges to it and deletes the owner lease. The healthy owner's next heartbeat finds no lease, and it shuts itself and its daemon down. The rule is not in the documented contract (`references/runtime/daemon-lease-contract.md` section 4 names a dead pid, a stale heartbeat or an unverifiable owner). An isolated reproduction with its own database showed the incumbent dead 25 s after a second n call, and scenario 433, which cold-starts a n on a sandbox socket against the live database, displaced the live advisor during the reruns at 22:25Z and 22:41Z.\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/009-test-findings-remediation/spec.md:| REQ-008 | A second n never displaces a live owner | A n test shows a live owner with a fresh heartbeat and parent pid 1 stays `live-owner`, and the isolated two-n reproduction keeps the incumbent alive past two heartbeat intervals |\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/013-review-follow-ups/evidence/reruns/reports/codex-CP-004.md:| 4 | Native n with 0.2-minute idle timeout | 0 | `status: ok`, `freshness: live`; `cold-start exit=0`. | Sandbox daemon returns an OK envelope with live freshness and exit 0. | Yes |\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/009-test-findings-remediation/tasks.md:- [x] T014 F17: the daemon child reaches the model server the n arms (`.skilled/bin/system-skill-advisor-n.cjs`), after T012 verifies. Evidence: n tests 38 of 38 with two new; with the new block removed, 4 tests fail.\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/009-test-findings-remediation/tasks.md:- [x] T022 F21: a second n never reclaims a live, heartbeating owner for its parent pid (`.skilled/bin/system-skill-advisor-n.cjs`, `runtime/tests/n-reap-pid-reuse.vitest.ts`). Evidence: the four n test files 47 of 47 outside the Codex sandbox; with the base n restored, the new test fails on `ownerLeaseReclaimed: ppid-1-orphan` and `expected true to be false`; the test's sleeper has parent pid 1. The isolated two-n reproduction killed the incumbent by t+25 s before the fix and kept it alive through t+60 s after it, with the live n pid and live files unchanged. Luna's diff, caller and test-shape checks passed. Its gate hit the Codex sandbox's socket and `ps` limits, so the orchestrator ran that gate.\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/011-observation-fixes/evidence/goal-reverify/reports/pi-433.md:NOTES: Teardown output verbatim: `sandbox n 34895 stopped` then `live generation file unchanged` — the lease file existed and its socket path was inside the sandbox, so the sandbox n (PID 34895) was TERMed and confirmed stopped; no live daemon or database was touched. The default-budget run landed on the `outage (fail_open)` variant (2508 ms, under the ~5 s cold window) rather than `live`/`stale`; the scenario file states all three variants are a pass. Hook diagnostic JSON went to stderr, stdout held only the JSON payloads — stdout was non-empty in all three runs. The sandbox dir was removed and no repository files were written. Flakiness: none; single run, first attempt.\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\":\"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research && rg -rn \\\"cold start|COLD_START_WAIT|daemon start|launcher\\\" .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/*.md .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/*.md specs/system-skill-advisor --glob '*.md' 2>/dev/null | grep -i \\\"ms\\\\|second\\\\|cold\\\" | head -20\"}"},"time":{"start":1794523914746,"end":1794523916416}}}}
