{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794524512589,"sessionID":"ses_e2e0e4d38ffeP1JOC1np1mhw9O","part":{"partID":"prt_1d1feb259001oQIJevlgQWuwpm","sessionID":"ses_e2e0e4d38ffeP1JOC1np1mhw9O","messageID":"msg_1d1fea77e001HrWqwb7CQafYlG","type":"tool","id":"call_ec43a9f116bb42d3827936d8","tool":"write","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"# Iteration 3: Token cost of the brief, the command read and the ask turn\n\n## Focus\nToken cost. The reducer's Next Focus still pointed at the p95 tail, which iteration 2 closed with a bounded answer, so this iteration follows the highest-value unchecked key question instead and records the tail follow-up as exhausted. It measures the brief per prompt and per session under phase 005's head form, the command document read that a `start` policy causes across the 44 tracked commands, and the turn cost of an `ask`.\n\n## Actions Taken\n1. Rendered the real brief from the shipped renderer with a live recommendation, to get exact characters rather than an estimate.\n2. Measured all 44 tracked command documents by path and size with `git ls-files` plus a stat pass, excluding assets and fixtures.\n3. Re-read phase 005's six golden heads and phase 006's measured policy mix.\n4. Computed a 40-prompt session with the current directive-lifecycle dedup and with phase 005's command head.\n5. Re-read the hook's dedup comment to confirm the directive block is dropped after the proven-repeat boundary.\n\n## Findings\n1. The shipped renderer produces a 257-character brief at the normal cap: a 41-character head plus a 216-character directive block. At the repository's own 4-characters-per-token estimate that is 64 tokens, and 54 of them are the constant comment-hygiene block. The constant block is 84% of the brief. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/render.ts:100] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/render.ts:162] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/render.ts:557]\n\n2. Phase 005's command head grows the line from about 41 to about 68 characters, so a full brief goes from 257 to 284 characters and from 64 to 71 tokens. A 40-prompt session with one full brief and 39 reduced heads costs about 466 tokens today and about 734 with the command, a 268-token delta or 0.37% of a 200,000-token context. The token cost of the surfaced command itself is not a performance problem. [SOURCE: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/005-recommendation-contract-and-brief/plan.md:71] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/render.ts:557]\n\n3. One started command costs far more than the brief: the 44 tracked command documents have a median of 4,524 bytes (about 1,131 tokens), a p95 of 12,573 bytes (about 3,143 tokens) and a maximum of 13,682 bytes (about 3,421 tokens). The five heaviest are `/deep:research` 13,682 B, `/speckit:handoff` 13,476 B, `/doctor:overlay` 12,573 B, `/speckit:save` 12,453 B and `/deep:review` 11,113 B. Three starts in a session cost about 3,393 tokens, 4.6 times that session's whole brief cost. [SOURCE: .skilled/commands/deep/research.md:1] [SOURCE: .skilled/commands/speckit/handoff.md:1] [SOURCE: .skilled/commands/doctor/overlay.md:1] [SOURCE: .skilled/commands/speckit/save.md:1]\n\n4. The largest token win in the brief is already built: the directive-lifecycle dedup delivers the full brief on the first message and after a lifecycle boundary or transcript shrink, then keeps the head and drops the constant block on proven repeats. That is the 84% saving, not a future optimization. A recommendation that trims the head would win at most 54 tokens per session. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.ts:373] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.ts:397]\n\n5. The policy mix is 20 `start`, 17 `ask` and 7 `run`. An `ask` costs one extra model turn per offered command: the model asks, the user answers, then the document read happens. That turn is the only material per-start cost, and it is a wall-time and attention cost rather than a token one. Phase 007 counts `COMMAND:` states but not the turn count, so the cost is currently invisible. [SOURCE: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/006-gate-2-binding-and-runtime-pointers/spec.md:94] [SOURCE: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/007-per-runtime-behaviour-proof/plan.md:68]\n\n6. The honest token conclusion is a negative result: the brief is 64 to 71 tokens, the dedup already removes 84% of it, and a started command's document read is the real cost at about 1,131 tokens median. The only token guard worth building is visibility of that read cost, so a future command document cannot silently ship at three times the p95. [INFERENCE: findings 1 to 4 compared against finding 3]\n\n## Ruled Out\n- Trimming the brief head or the directive block as a token saving. The head is 41 characters and the block is already suppressed on repeats; the ceiling is 54 tokens per session.\n- A per-prompt token budget mechanism. The 80-token cap already binds and the measured brief is 64 tokens.\n- Caching rendered briefs to save tokens. Rendering costs well under a millisecond and tokens are charged for context, not render time.\n- Following the reducer's p95-tail pointer again. Iteration 2 answered it with a bounded variance claim; re-investigating it would repeat exhausted work.\n\n## Dead Ends\n- No per-start token measurement exists in any phase packet. Phase 007 measures whether a command starts, never what the start costs. The doc-size measurement here stands in for it; a live token count from a started session would need a runtime run, which the steer forbids.\n\n## Edge Cases\n- Ambiguous input: \"token cost of the brief\" could mean the whole delivered context or the brief alone. Measured the brief alone; the command document read is reported separately because it dominates.\n- Contradictory evidence: the UX angle may argue for a longer, richer brief while this angle finds the brief is negligible in tokens. Both can hold; the measurement does not decide UX.\n- Missing dependencies: no tokenizer is available locally, so tokens are the repository's own 4-characters-per-token estimate, consistent with the renderer's cap. Recorded rather than guessed as exact.\n- Partial success: findings 1 to 5 are measured or read from code; finding 6 is synthesis. Status is `complete`.\n\n## Sources Consulted\n- .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/render.ts:100,162,557\n- .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.ts:373-427\n- specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/005-recommendation-contract-and-brief/plan.md:67-79\n- specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/006-gate-2-binding-and-runtime-pointers/spec.md:94\n- specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/007-per-runtime-behaviour-proof/plan.md:65-69\n- Command output: rendered brief 257 chars, 41-char head, 216-char directive block\n- Command output: 44 command docs, median 4,524 B, p95 12,573 B, max 13,682 B, total 254,463 B\n\n## Assessment\n- New information ratio: 0.90\n- Questions addressed: token cost per prompt, per session and per started command; the ask turn\n- Questions answered: the token-cost key question\n\n## Reflection\n- What worked and why: rendering the real brief instead of estimating it produced exact numbers and killed a plausible but false optimization axis. Measuring the command documents turned \"a command read\" into a distribution with a p95 and a top five.\n- What did not work and why: no tokenizer exists, so the numbers carry the renderer's own estimate. That is acceptable for a 4x decision but not for a billing claim, and it is stated.\n- What I would do differently: measure the doc sizes before reading the dedup code, because the dedup makes the brief's constant block irrelevant and would have shortened the analysis.\n\n## SCOPE VIOLATIONS\nNone. No out-of-scope write was attempted.\n\n## Recommended Next Focus\nCache and precompute across the six runtimes: what the exact prompt cache, the OpenCode plugin cache and the directive dedup actually save at a realistic hit rate, whether a persistent or semantic cache is worth it, and how the per-runtime architectures (Claude 3 processes, OpenCode/Pi/DSH 2, Codex/Devin/Cursor pointer-only) compare per prompt.\n","path":"specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/009-ux-performance-cross-cli-research/research/lineages/performance/iterations/iteration-003.md"},"output":"Created file successfully: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/009-ux-performance-cross-cli-research/research/lineages/performance/iterations/iteration-003.md","title":"write","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Created file successfully: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/009-ux-performance-cross-cli-research/research/lineages/performance/iterations/iteration-003.md"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"content\":\"# Iteration 3: Token cost of the brief, the command read and the ask turn\\n\\n## Focus\\nToken cost. The reducer's Next Focus still pointed at the p95 tail, which iteration 2 closed with a bounded answer, so this iteration follows the highest-value unchecked key question instead and records the tail follow-up as exhausted. It measures the brief per prompt and per session under phase 005's head form, the command document read that a `start` policy causes across the 44 tracked commands, and the turn cost of an `ask`.\\n\\n## Actions Taken\\n1. Rendered the real brief from the shipped renderer with a live recommendation, to get exact characters rather than an estimate.\\n2. Measured all 44 tracked command documents by path and size with `git ls-files` plus a stat pass, excluding assets and fixtures.\\n3. Re-read phase 005's six golden heads and phase 006's measured policy mix.\\n4. Computed a 40-prompt session with the current directive-lifecycle dedup and with phase 005's command head.\\n5. Re-read the hook's dedup comment to confirm the directive block is dropped after the proven-repeat boundary.\\n\\n## Findings\\n1. The shipped renderer produces a 257-character brief at the normal cap: a 41-character head plus a 216-character directive block. At the repository's own 4-characters-per-token estimate that is 64 tokens, and 54 of them are the constant comment-hygiene block. The constant block is 84% of the brief. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/render.ts:100] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/render.ts:162] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/render.ts:557]\\n\\n2. Phase 005's command head grows the line from about 41 to about 68 characters, so a full brief goes from 257 to 284 characters and from 64 to 71 tokens. A 40-prompt session with one full brief and 39 reduced heads costs about 466 tokens today and about 734 with the command, a 268-token delta or 0.37% of a 200,000-token context. The token cost of the surfaced command itself is not a performance problem. [SOURCE: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/005-recommendation-contract-and-brief/plan.md:71] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/render.ts:557]\\n\\n3. One started command costs far more than the brief: the 44 tracked command documents have a median of 4,524 bytes (about 1,131 tokens), a p95 of 12,573 bytes (about 3,143 tokens) and a maximum of 13,682 bytes (about 3,421 tokens). The five heaviest are `/deep:research` 13,682 B, `/speckit:handoff` 13,476 B, `/doctor:overlay` 12,573 B, `/speckit:save` 12,453 B and `/deep:review` 11,113 B. Three starts in a session cost about 3,393 tokens, 4.6 times that session's whole brief cost. [SOURCE: .skilled/commands/deep/research.md:1] [SOURCE: .skilled/commands/speckit/handoff.md:1] [SOURCE: .skilled/commands/doctor/overlay.md:1] [SOURCE: .skilled/commands/speckit/save.md:1]\\n\\n4. The largest token win in the brief is already built: the directive-lifecycle dedup delivers the full brief on the first message and after a lifecycle boundary or transcript shrink, then keeps the head and drops the constant block on proven repeats. That is the 84% saving, not a future optimization. A recommendation that trims the head would win at most 54 tokens per session. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.ts:373] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.ts:397]\\n\\n5. The policy mix is 20 `start`, 17 `ask` and 7 `run`. An `ask` costs one extra model turn per offered command: the model asks, the user answers, then the document read happens. That turn is the only material per-start cost, and it is a wall-time and attention cost rather than a token one. Phase 007 counts `COMMAND:` states but not the turn count, so the cost is currently invisible. [SOURCE: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/006-gate-2-binding-and-runtime-pointers/spec.md:94] [SOURCE: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/007-per-runtime-behaviour-proof/plan.md:68]\\n\\n6. The honest token conclusion is a negative result: the brief is 64 to 71 tokens, the dedup already removes 84% of it, and a started command's document read is the real cost at about 1,131 tokens median. The only token guard worth building is visibility of that read cost, so a future command document cannot silently ship at three times the p95. [INFERENCE: findings 1 to 4 compared against finding 3]\\n\\n## Ruled Out\\n- Trimming the brief head or the directive block as a token saving. The head is 41 characters and the block is already suppressed on repeats; the ceiling is 54 tokens per session.\\n- A per-prompt token budget mechanism. The 80-token cap already binds and the measured brief is 64 tokens.\\n- Caching rendered briefs to save tokens. Rendering costs well under a millisecond and tokens are charged for context, not render time.\\n- Following the reducer's p95-tail pointer again. Iteration 2 answered it with a bounded variance claim; re-investigating it would repeat exhausted work.\\n\\n## Dead Ends\\n- No per-start token measurement exists in any phase packet. Phase 007 measures whether a command starts, never what the start costs. The doc-size measurement here stands in for it; a live token count from a started session would need a runtime run, which the steer forbids.\\n\\n## Edge Cases\\n- Ambiguous input: \\\"token cost of the brief\\\" could mean the whole delivered context or the brief alone. Measured the brief alone; the command document read is reported separately because it dominates.\\n- Contradictory evidence: the UX angle may argue for a longer, richer brief while this angle finds the brief is negligible in tokens. Both can hold; the measurement does not decide UX.\\n- Missing dependencies: no tokenizer is available locally, so tokens are the repository's own 4-characters-per-token estimate, consistent with the renderer's cap. Recorded rather than guessed as exact.\\n- Partial success: findings 1 to 5 are measured or read from code; finding 6 is synthesis. Status is `complete`.\\n\\n## Sources Consulted\\n- .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/render.ts:100,162,557\\n- .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.ts:373-427\\n- specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/005-recommendation-contract-and-brief/plan.md:67-79\\n- specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/006-gate-2-binding-and-runtime-pointers/spec.md:94\\n- specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/007-per-runtime-behaviour-proof/plan.md:65-69\\n- Command output: rendered brief 257 chars, 41-char head, 216-char directive block\\n- Command output: 44 command docs, median 4,524 B, p95 12,573 B, max 13,682 B, total 254,463 B\\n\\n## Assessment\\n- New information ratio: 0.90\\n- Questions addressed: token cost per prompt, per session and per started command; the ask turn\\n- Questions answered: the token-cost key question\\n\\n## Reflection\\n- What worked and why: rendering the real brief instead of estimating it produced exact numbers and killed a plausible but false optimization axis. Measuring the command documents turned \\\"a command read\\\" into a distribution with a p95 and a top five.\\n- What did not work and why: no tokenizer exists, so the numbers carry the renderer's own estimate. That is acceptable for a 4x decision but not for a billing claim, and it is stated.\\n- What I would do differently: measure the doc sizes before reading the dedup code, because the dedup makes the brief's constant block irrelevant and would have shortened the analysis.\\n\\n## SCOPE VIOLATIONS\\nNone. No out-of-scope write was attempted.\\n\\n## Recommended Next Focus\\nCache and precompute across the six runtimes: what the exact prompt cache, the OpenCode plugin cache and the directive dedup actually save at a realistic hit rate, whether a persistent or semantic cache is worth it, and how the per-runtime architectures (Claude 3 processes, OpenCode/Pi/DSH 2, Codex/Devin/Cursor pointer-only) compare per prompt.\\n\",\"path\":\"specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/009-ux-performance-cross-cli-research/research/lineages/performance/iterations/iteration-003.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794524492377,"end":1794524512589}}}}
